  ],
  "max_blocks": 20,
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "t:sections.story-content.name",
      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "our story",
        "heading": "We inherited extraordinary. And forgot it.",
        "intro": "<p>India gave the world the Kamasutra. A philosophy of desire, presence, and intimacy as art. Somewhere along the way, we decided to be ashamed of it. Our ancestors built that knowledge deliberately, and treated it with reverence.</p>"
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "number": "01",
            "eyebrow": "why we exist",
            "heading": "the problem no one was talking about.",
            "body": "<p>The Kamasutra was never just about sex positions. It was a science of relationships. A guide to understanding our own desires, your partner's body, and the art of being fully present.</p><p>Our ancestors built that knowledge deliberately, and treated it with reverence. We live in its birthplace and know almost none of it.</p><p>The result is chronic dissatisfaction: partners feeling disconnected, people carrying shame around their own pleasure, and an entire conversation that never happens.</p>",
            "pull_quote": "We live in its birthplace and know almost none of it. This is the problem we were built to solve."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "quote_cta",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "Our ancestors had answers. We're just translating them.",
            "attribution": "Pillowta Founders",
            "button_text": "Join the community"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "number": "02",
            "heading": "why ancient wisdom.",
            "body": "<p>We didn't need to import a framework for sex. Intimacy wasn't always reduced to technique or outcome. There was a time when it was understood as something deeper, something to be explored, not rushed.</p><p>Our own tradition, which saw sex as science, poetry, and self-knowledge, was quietly buried. The Kamasutra was not lost. It was colonised out of our consciousness.</p><p>Pillowta exists to bring it back. Not as a museum exhibit, but as living, practical wisdom for the problems of modern intimacy.</p>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "highlight",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Living, Not Museum",
            "text": "<p>We're not preserving history. We're translating it into something you can use today.</p>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "highlight",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Science of Relationships",
            "text": "<p>The Kamasutra codified desire, presence, and mutual understanding centuries before modern psychology existed.</p>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "quote_cta",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "Our ancestors had answers. We're just translating them.",
            "attribution": "Pillowta Founders",
            "button_text": "Join the community"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
